  

<h2 id="plain-text-slop-soup-anyone">Plain Text Slop Soup, Anyone?</h2>
<p>I was initially excited to discover a new plain text productivity site this week, with sections on open format apps and tips for plain text workflows. Hooray! I thought. Another resource to add to my <a href="https://ellanew.com/friends" class="wikilink">friends</a>!</p>
<p>But a few seconds in, things began to feel off.</p>
<p>It didn’t take long to realise that I was looking at one of the internet’s newest claims to shame, the content farm: a site stuffed full of keywords that exists primarily to make money from topics already clocking significant amounts of traffic.</p>
<p>These<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">‘</span>farms’ are always monetised and often use low quality ad partners who consider your rights to privacy and a good user experience, irrelevant. The content is usually superficial and can reasonably be assumed to be AI-generated.</p>
<p>I am writing with one specific site in mind, but this is a name-and-educate, not a name-and-shame. You don’t need to know who they are, but this is how they present themselves:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[Site name redacted] is your comprehensive resource for everything related to text editors and note-taking apps across all major operating systems. Explore in-depth guides, comparisons, and how-tos covering built-in editors like Notepad and TextEdit, powerful third-party apps, plain text workflows, and productivity tips for Windows, macOS, Linux, Android, and iOS.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sounds good, right?</p>
<p>But attempts to explore the site are hamstrung by pop-up ads that cover half the screen and can’t be permanently dismissed. The little I saw of their content showed it to be summaries of what most people interested in the topic already know. Nothing wrong with that, but I was hoping for experiences of the author/s woven throughout. Nope. It was all highly third-person impersonal.</p>
<p>All they tell you about who’s behind the site is that it is run by team of<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>dedicated writers and researchers committed to delivering accurate, helpful, and engaging content to our readers.”</p>
<p>The biggest red flag of them all: several lower-than-low quality pop-under tabs appeared on my browser after clicking on the link to contact them. Boo.</p>
<p>The only reason I stayed there as long as I did, was the topic. I admit that flying the plain text flag lent them more of my attention than I’d usually give a site like this.</p>
<p>I’ve seen content farms before but this is the first time I’ve come across one with a plain text theme. Who knew that my niche interest would garner that kind of popularity! I should be happy, I guess? But the truth is that it’s sad to see this topic tainted by slop-producing money-grabbers.</p>
<p>I’ve written to them with my concerns but unsurprisingly, haven’t heard back. Should they contact me I will update this piece.</p>
<h3 id="what-now">What now?</h3>
<p>As much as it grates on me, I recognise that plain text topics catching the attention of content farmers is a backhanded compliment. The word is spreading!</p>
<p>Those of us dedicated to digital freedom may not be able to stem the tide of <span class="small-caps">AI</span> slop, but we can keep on being human, flaws and all, and continue sharing what’s important to us. Let’s focus on human interactions and put helpful stuff out there with no expectation of fame or reward, because that’s what good humans do. <a href="https://commandline.johnnydecimal.com/learn/01-introduction-and-goals/" target="_blank">It’s what Johnny Decimal did for me</a>.</p>
<p>Plain text workflows and digital autonomy are my thing so I’m going to keep writing and sharing on those topics.</p>
<p>Let the slop flow, if flow it must; the more of it there is, the more those who are genuine will stand out. Bring back webrings and <a href="https://ellanew.com/friends" class="wikilink">blogrolls</a>! Real people* standing hand in hand across the ether as beacons of light, pointing the way out of the slop-fields.</p>
